\claim{List length and coverage lift both metrics.}
A reader that only knows whether the store covers the question scores $0.75$ pooled and $0.50$ matched.
The name lookup stays a reliable $0.13$ above a lookup over meaningless tokens on the matched metric. (Appendix~\ref{app:counterfactual})

\claim{Backends omit or misstate the lifecycle information readers need.}
FR-Bank marks about $58$ times as many entries stale as ground truth, yet retains obsolete assertions as active and conflates retraction with supersession.
Mem0 overwrites at the retrieval surface we evaluated, and A-MEM's links resolve to no note in our integration (Table~\ref{tab:census}). (Appendix~\ref{app:census})

\claim{FR-Bank's own tags help mini despite over-marking.}
Its view-only score rises reliably from $0.69$ to $0.81$ with native tags, whose precision over retrieved lines is $0.14$, because they mark more of the forbidding records already in view. A reader that merely counts tagged lines scores $0.62$ under either tag source.
On Graphiti, native tags on the same retrieved edges bring no reliable improvement. (Appendix~\ref{app:posthoc-graphiti})

\claim{Keeping the newest record misses every tested retraction obligation.}
At the matched budget, newest-per-attribute retrieval reaches $0.23$ of forbidding records overall and zero of $26$ retraction obligations.
The cancellation is not the newest record of the attribute it cancels. (Appendix~\ref{app:resp})

\claim{Full-set models do not reliably beat the lookup.}
On the full obligation set, Haiku, Luna and Opus score $0.82$ to $0.83$, against the lookup's $0.75$; their margins fail the persona-grouped interval.
On easier coverage questions, mini's advantage over the superseded-name lookup disappears against a lookup over the whole name map. (Appendix~\ref{app:baselines})
